Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  an uneasy peace between china and taiwan  has existed for more than 70 years  that peace is now under unusual strain  taiwan is a self-governed island of 24  million people with a thriving democracy  but the chinese communist party claims  that it's part of china  which is why when nancy pelosi the  speaker of america's house of  representatives visited taiwan  china flexed its military muscles  a real collapse in trust and it's a  really dangerous moment  tensions are high  the question is  will it lead to war  it's really striking out the last few  years in places like washington other  capitals there is a new sense of urgency  about how soon chinese communist leaders  particularly xi jinping could feel they  have no choice but to use force in some  way to take taiwan back war over taiwan  cannot possibly be inevitable if you're  chinese and you think you can do it  another way you've got to want to do it  without fighting because it would be  unbelievably costly to so much that  china has built up over the last decades  of economic prosperity it would also be  unimaginably dangerous if you think the  ukraine crisis is scary remember that  joe biden made clear on day one that  american soldiers were never going to  fight russian soldiers that's not the  case in taiwan there are plenty of  realistic scenarios that on day one of a  taiwan war the american military would  have to sink chinese ships as they tried  to invade taiwan that the chinese air  force might have to bomb american  fighter jets sitting in japanese air  force bases that is very very close to a  regional if not a world war and they're  nuclear superpowers it should be utterly  unthinkable why is it not unthinkable  because if you're the leader of the  chinese communist party and you look at  taiwan the problem that you have is that  time works in two very different ways  with every passing year china's military  gets stronger and the taiwanese military  gets weaker and china's ability to keep  america at bay looks more impressive so  that could be a reason to be patient  think well why rush why take a risk of a  war that we could lose but the passage  of time also works another way which is  with every passing year hearts and minds  the people of taiwan particularly young  people in taiwan they become less and  less interested in being run from  beijing by the communist party fewer and  fewer taiwanese think of themselves as  chinese in any meaningful sense there  are worst case scenarios where china  could feel it had no choice but to stop  taiwan from declaring independence or  drifting away where xi jinping a man  with a very strong sense of his own  destiny could feel i have to do this  before i step down and that really  worries uh leaders and generals in the  west i started an american official who  studied this  this crisis for years and he said the  problem is we've never seen this level  of military build up this level of  nationalism not end in a conflict we  shouldn't pretend that every american  has strong views about the future of  taiwan but in certain key places above  all congress in washington democrats and  republicans who don't agree on much they  agree that taiwan is a plucky  pro-american pro-western democracy that  deserves to be protected from the  communist bully next door and that's a  very powerful bipartisan message and  there is there's pressure on the  administration to do more to help taiwan  pressure that china sees as a hostile  offensive move and so the american  foreign policy elite the american  political classes they are more  pro-taiwan than we've seen for a very  long time and that is a very  destabilizing situation here's the  danger taiwanese public opinion  is remarkably calm in the face of this  crisis and one of the things we've heard  during the nancy pelosi visit is you  know why is the western media so up in  arms about this uh you know we taiwanese  are so calm we don't get fussed about  these attacks china makes too much money  uh in taiwan to ever do something stupid  like start a war that's a really common  line to hear in taiwan  but actually there are plenty of people  in america who think that it's really  possible that these threats are real and  that they could have to fight a war  there is no way that americans can care  more about taiwan's safety than the  taiwanese do so if there is a sense in  america that taiwanese public opinion is  actually complacent and that young  taiwanese are just not interested in  looking after themselves in joining the  army in doing real national service then  i think you could see support for taiwan  start to waver i am really struck in my  time as a china correspondent you know  it used to be that grown-up  sophisticated ambassadors senior  officials in governments like the  american government who had access to  the most classified intelligence if you  ask them could there be a war over  taiwan  not that long ago the kind of  sophisticated  knowing thing to say was of course not  it's unthinkable you know the stakes are  too high china knows it's too dangerous  and now i hear very different messages  it's maybe and maybe quite soon maybe  before the end of this decade and  there's a real soberness about uh how  dangerous the stakes are and how the two  sides are absolutely convinced that the  status quo is being undermined by the  other there is a real collapse in trust  and it's a really dangerous moment  i'm david rennie beijing buruchi for the  economist if you'd like to read more of  our coverage of china and taiwan please  click the link  thank you for watching and don't forget  to subscribe

